<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesrop Mashtots</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 362–440</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Saint Mesrop</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesrop Mashtots was an Armenian monk and scholar who created the Armenian alphabet around 405 with the support of King Vramshapuh and the Catholicos Sahak. With his pupils he translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and other Christian texts, beginning a golden age of Armenian literature. Armenian tradition also credits him with alphabets for Georgian and Caucasian Albanian. He is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesrop-mashtots.jpg" alt="Mesrop Mashtots" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stepanos Nersisyan (1815-84) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mesrop_Mashtots_1882_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mesrop Mashtots was born in the village of Hatsekats in the canton of Taron. His father was named Vardan; scholars have taken him for a priest, a nobleman, a member of the Mamikonian clan or a peasant. His pupil and biographer Koriun says he received a Greek education, and he also knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>. In the late 380s he moved to the Armenian capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a>, and worked at the royal court, where sources give him posts including royal chancellor. Around 394 he became a monk and a hermit, then gathered 40 disciples and, with the blessing of the Catholicos Sahak Partev, began missionary work among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, many of whom were still pagan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> had lost its independence in 387 and was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Armenians read Christian texts in Greek, Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, which suited the sounds of their language poorly, so scripture and liturgy were largely unintelligible to the faithful. Mashtots, helped by Sahak and King Vramshapuh, and after consulting the bishop Daniel and the monk Rufinus, created an alphabet of thirty-six letters around 405; two more were added in the twelfth century. He founded schools to teach it and sent pupils to Edessa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and other centres to study Greek and bring back texts.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> was translated, the version in use being completed about 434, and the decrees of the first three ecumenical councils and many works of the Greek Fathers followed. Medieval Armenian sources also credit him with the Georgian and Caucasian Albanian alphabets; this is disputed, and most scholars link the Georgian script to the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mashtots survived Sahak by about six months and died on 17 February 440. He is buried at a chapel in Oshakan. The Armenian Apostolic Church keeps two feasts for him, and pilgrims visit his grave. Armenian writers treat him as the founder of Armenian literature and education and a symbol of the language, church and school system; Koriun compared his return after inventing the alphabet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> descending from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a>. Soviet historians presented him as a secular figure, and the Catholicos Karekin I objected in 1991 that his work was described in purely political terms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesrop Mashtots” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A church organisation developed with its own catholicos, closely tied to the Armenian Church, and in the early fifth century an alphabet was created for the Albanian language, which Armenian tradition credits to <b>Mesrop Mashtots</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>That later line made Armenia, in 301 or 314, the first state in history to adopt Christianity officially, and gave it the alphabet of <b>Mesrop Mashtots</b> in 405.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their written language begins with the alphabet of <b>Mesrop Mashtots</b> in 405 and a 5th-century translation of the Bible, its oldest surviving text.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ulfilas most likely invented the Gothic alphabet for his Bible; Cyril and Methodius devised Glagolitic for their mission to Moravia in 862 or 863; <b>Mesrop Mashtots</b> created the Armenian alphabet of thirty-six letters in 405; King Sejong of Korea promulgated Hangul in 1446.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 405 <b>Mesrop Mashtots</b> created the Armenian alphabet, launching a translation literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hatsekats. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.17,44.29&amp;year=412" aria-label="Vagharshapat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Mesrop Mashtots"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5924 4077h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5924 4077h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.17,44.29&amp;year=412">What was here in 412 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
